1
00:00:00,620 --> 00:00:04,140
Okay, moving along, the next section is the cervix.

2
00:00:04,140 --> 00:00:06,260
Remember that the cervix is the neck,

3
00:00:06,260 --> 00:00:08,560
hence the term cervix, of the uterus,

4
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:12,240
and it's divided into the exocervix and the endocervix.

5
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:14,500
Now the idea here is that when you do a clinical exam,

6
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:17,220
you can actually visualize the exocervix,

7
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:19,680
and sometimes you can visualize the endocervix.

8
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:21,600
What's important histologically is that

9
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:23,380
when you look at the exo and endocervix,

10
00:00:23,380 --> 00:00:26,960
there's a very distinct demarcation between them,

11
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:29,380
and there are differing types of epithelium

12
00:00:29,380 --> 00:00:30,700
that cover them.

13
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:33,060
For example, if this is a basement membrane

14
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:35,880
and we're drawing the exocervix and the endocervix,

15
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:38,060
then remember that the exocervix,

16
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:39,600
let's say that's here,

17
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:44,040
the exocervix is lined by squamous epithelium,

18
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:45,160
all right, like that.

19
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:47,300
And so this would be the exocervix.

20
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:52,660
And then the endocervix is lined by columnar epithelium.

21
00:00:52,660 --> 00:00:55,600
And the distinction between the exo and the endocervix

22
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:57,860
is so sharp that it's actually a single cell

23
00:00:57,860 --> 00:01:00,540
that you can actually sort of make that distinction between.

24
00:01:00,540 --> 00:01:02,540
It's very, very clear on histology,

25
00:01:02,540 --> 00:01:06,540
and that lends to a very clear demarcation clinically as well.

26
00:01:06,540 --> 00:01:08,540
Here's a picture just highlighting that.

27
00:01:08,540 --> 00:01:11,180
I just want to show you how sharp this demarcation is.

28
00:01:11,180 --> 00:01:13,180
This is the squamous epithelium here,

29
00:01:13,180 --> 00:01:15,540
and you can see this basket weaving of keratin

30
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:19,540
that's occurring as we get to the top of the epithelium.

31
00:01:19,540 --> 00:01:23,220
And so this is the exocervix here.

32
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:25,220
And then you can clearly see that here,

33
00:01:25,220 --> 00:01:28,220
you've got all these individual columnar cells,

34
00:01:28,220 --> 00:01:30,220
and so this represents the endocervix.

35
00:01:30,220 --> 00:01:33,220
Now, important to note that this region here

36
00:01:33,220 --> 00:01:34,220
that I'm drawing the arrow towards,

37
00:01:34,220 --> 00:01:36,220
where that demarcation occurs,

38
00:01:36,220 --> 00:01:38,220
that's called the transformation zone.

39
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:41,220
And the whole story of what we're going to discuss in the cervix

40
00:01:41,220 --> 00:01:45,220
is its story of what happens at the transformation zone.

41
00:01:45,220 --> 00:01:48,900
The only real pathology that we're going to discuss in this section,

42
00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:50,420
sort of the highest yield,

43
00:01:50,420 --> 00:01:52,460
and really the most important clinically as well,

44
00:01:52,460 --> 00:01:53,900
is HPV infection.

45
00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:55,900
Now, remember in our discussion of the vulva

46
00:01:55,900 --> 00:01:57,540
that I told you a lot about HPV.

47
00:01:57,540 --> 00:02:00,300
So HPV is characterized by coelocytic change.

48
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:02,140
HPV is a DNA virus.

49
00:02:02,140 --> 00:02:05,620
You can subtype that DNA virus using DNA sequencing

50
00:02:05,620 --> 00:02:07,540
into high-risk and low-risk.

51
00:02:07,540 --> 00:02:09,740
Low-risk, for example, 6 and 11,

52
00:02:09,740 --> 00:02:11,900
classically causes a condyloma.

53
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:14,140
High-risk, for example, 16, 18,

54
00:02:14,140 --> 00:02:16,540
and then 31, 33, and some others,

55
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:18,320
would result in dysplasia.

56
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:21,320
And then dysplasia would eventually result in carcinoma.

57
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:23,620
And so that's the basics that you really have to have

58
00:02:23,620 --> 00:02:26,220
well embedded in your mind concerning HPV.

59
00:02:26,220 --> 00:02:27,960
And that's what I basically stated here.

60
00:02:27,960 --> 00:02:30,560
It's a sexually transmitted DNA virus.

61
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:32,260
It infects the lower genital tract.

62
00:02:32,260 --> 00:02:34,240
For example, if it infects the vulva,

63
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:37,600
you get condyloma or dysplasia.

64
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:39,540
If it infects the vaginal canal,

65
00:02:39,540 --> 00:02:41,700
you can get condyloma or dysplasia.

66
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:42,840
And if it affects the cervix,

67
00:02:42,840 --> 00:02:45,120
you get condyloma or dysplasia.

68
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:47,340
And one of the most common regions

69
00:02:47,340 --> 00:02:50,580
within the entire lower genital tract

70
00:02:50,580 --> 00:02:51,620
would be the cervix,

71
00:02:51,620 --> 00:02:53,720
especially the transformation zone.

72
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:54,460
And that's important.

73
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:57,600
The transformation zone is the key place

74
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:00,920
in the lower genital tract where HPV likes to infect.

75
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:03,660
Now, what's another important interesting thing about HPV

76
00:03:03,660 --> 00:03:05,840
is that when we get an HPV infection,

77
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:07,940
if someone were to get an HPV infection,

78
00:03:07,940 --> 00:03:09,840
the vast majority of HPV infection

79
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:11,420
is actually cleared by the immune system.

80
00:03:11,420 --> 00:03:13,340
Probably about 90% of the time

81
00:03:13,340 --> 00:03:15,340
when anyone gets an HPV infection,

82
00:03:15,340 --> 00:03:17,920
that's just gonna be cleared by the immune system.

83
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:20,280
It's only when persistent infection occurs,

84
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:22,800
which is relatively rare considering the number

85
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:24,560
of infections out there.

86
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:27,580
When persistent infection occurs,

87
00:03:27,580 --> 00:03:29,780
then a risk results for CIN.

88
00:03:29,780 --> 00:03:33,140
And so, again, you need persistent infection.

89
00:03:33,140 --> 00:03:34,840
Now, I've already mentioned,

90
00:03:34,840 --> 00:03:36,240
and I'm just going to repeat here,

91
00:03:36,240 --> 00:03:39,480
that the risk of CIN with persistent infection

92
00:03:39,480 --> 00:03:41,020
depends on the type of HPV.

93
00:03:41,020 --> 00:03:43,980
So, for example, high risks are underlined here

94
00:03:43,980 --> 00:03:46,080
and low risks are underlined here.

95
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:47,260
And I've already discussed this,

96
00:03:47,260 --> 00:03:49,320
and it's already written in your book as well.

97
00:03:49,320 --> 00:03:53,060
What is very high yield, and I cannot emphasize this enough,

98
00:03:53,060 --> 00:03:55,260
and students need to be able to take this discussion

99
00:03:55,260 --> 00:03:57,060
one step further to this point,

100
00:03:57,060 --> 00:04:00,860
and that is, what makes high risk HPV high risk?

101
00:04:00,860 --> 00:04:04,100
What is it about the virus that actually makes it high risk?

102
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:05,840
And the answer is that high risk HPVs,

103
00:04:05,840 --> 00:04:08,380
they produce two proteins, E6 and E7.

104
00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:09,900
And what do E6 and E7 do?

105
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:13,280
They knock out P53 and retinoblastoma respectively.

106
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:16,640
So, when you have E6 being produced by the HPV virus,

107
00:04:16,640 --> 00:04:19,640
and the high risk HPV virus, that then results

108
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:21,180
in the destruction of P53.

109
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:23,240
And you're well aware of the fact that P53

110
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:25,280
is an important tumor suppressor gene.

111
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:29,620
Remember that P53, its job is to regulate the G1 to S phase

112
00:04:29,620 --> 00:04:30,820
in the cell cycle.

113
00:04:30,820 --> 00:04:33,420
And it basically looks at the amount of DNA damage

114
00:04:33,420 --> 00:04:35,860
that's present in the cell and makes sure

115
00:04:35,860 --> 00:04:37,420
that the DNA damage is repaired

116
00:04:37,420 --> 00:04:39,400
before the cell goes to S phase.

117
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:41,660
Remember that if there's too much DNA damage,

118
00:04:41,660 --> 00:04:44,440
P53 has the ability to induce apoptosis.

119
00:04:44,440 --> 00:04:46,940
So, basically, P53 looks at the cell and says,

120
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:48,980
put your hands up, let me check your DNA.

121
00:04:48,980 --> 00:04:51,380
If the DNA has damage, it blows a molecular whistle

122
00:04:51,380 --> 00:04:53,180
and calls in repair enzymes.

123
00:04:53,180 --> 00:04:54,960
The repair enzymes then fix that DNA

124
00:04:54,960 --> 00:04:57,020
and the cell is allowed to go to S phase.

125
00:04:57,020 --> 00:05:00,180
If there's too much damage, P53 blows a molecular whistle,

126
00:05:00,180 --> 00:05:03,560
calls in a molecule called, do you remember the name?

127
00:05:03,560 --> 00:05:04,720
Bax.

128
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:08,500
Bax knocks out another molecule, which is BCL2.

129
00:05:08,500 --> 00:05:10,300
Remember that BCL2 is the molecule

130
00:05:10,300 --> 00:05:12,600
that stabilizes the mitochondrial membrane.

131
00:05:12,600 --> 00:05:14,900
So, that loss of stability of the mitochondrial membrane

132
00:05:14,900 --> 00:05:16,940
allows cytochrome C to leak out.

133
00:05:16,940 --> 00:05:20,340
Cytochrome C activates apoptosis and then kills that cell.

134
00:05:20,340 --> 00:05:25,120
So, that's the way by which P53 regulates that G1 to S phase.

135
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:26,940
And, of course, if you destroy P53,

136
00:05:26,940 --> 00:05:30,120
there's going to be no regulation to remove DNA mutations

137
00:05:30,120 --> 00:05:32,960
or cells that are mutated with their,

138
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:35,620
or cells in which the DNA is actually mutated.

139
00:05:35,620 --> 00:05:42,700
And so, therefore, loss of P53 would have a major effect on carcinogenesis.

140
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:47,240
And that's why high-risk HPV can result in dysplasia and then cancer.

141
00:05:47,240 --> 00:05:50,260
And E7, what it does is it knocks out retinoblastoma.

142
00:05:50,260 --> 00:05:53,440
And you recall that retinoblastoma holds a very important molecule,

143
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:55,400
which is called E2F.

144
00:05:55,400 --> 00:05:58,400
And E2F is necessary for progression to the cell cycle.

145
00:05:58,400 --> 00:06:02,200
You must phosphorylate retinoblastoma before it will release E2F.

146
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:06,140
If you destroy retinoblastoma, E2F is floating around free

147
00:06:06,140 --> 00:06:09,580
and therefore will be able to be taken up by the cell and moved,

148
00:06:09,580 --> 00:06:12,980
or used by the cell to move into the G1S phase.

149
00:06:12,980 --> 00:06:15,760
So, you need these two essential tumor suppressor genes.

150
00:06:15,760 --> 00:06:19,560
And the high-risk HPVs, they knock out both of these key genes.

151
00:06:19,560 --> 00:06:23,660
This is a very high-yield discussion for examinations.

152
00:06:23,660 --> 00:06:25,200
I cannot emphasize this enough.

153
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:27,160
It shows up all the time.

154
00:06:27,160 --> 00:06:31,000
All right, now, if a patient were to get persistent high-risk HPV,

155
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:33,000
the risk would be development of CIN.

156
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,400
And basically, CIN is dysplasia, bad growth,

157
00:06:37,400 --> 00:06:41,240
so that you get atypical cells with high NC ratio,

158
00:06:41,240 --> 00:06:44,320
increased mitotic activity, nuclear atypia,

159
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:47,480
and choilocytic change because there's an HPV infection.

160
00:06:47,480 --> 00:06:51,580
And these cells begin to pile up in the epithelium of the cervix.

161
00:06:51,580 --> 00:06:54,160
And based on the extent of these dysplastic cells,

162
00:06:54,160 --> 00:06:57,200
we can divide CIN into multiple subtypes.

163
00:06:57,200 --> 00:06:58,560
So, let's do that here.

164
00:06:58,560 --> 00:07:00,000
This is the cervix, okay?

165
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:08,080
And let's pretend that the cervix is made up of 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 cells,

166
00:07:08,080 --> 00:07:09,440
just as an example.

167
00:07:09,440 --> 00:07:13,280
If the dysplasia involves any one of these first three cells,

168
00:07:13,280 --> 00:07:16,280
that would be the first one-third, we would call that CIN 1.

169
00:07:16,280 --> 00:07:24,160
If the dysplasia progresses and extends beyond that to involve any cells within this middle two-thirds,

170
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:28,720
to now the first six cells in that layer, for example, we call that CIN 2.

171
00:07:28,720 --> 00:07:32,800
If the dysplasia extends up to involve most of that epithelium,

172
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:34,560
then we call that CIN 3.

173
00:07:34,560 --> 00:07:38,320
And if the dysplasia involves the entire thickness of the epithelium,

174
00:07:38,320 --> 00:07:40,400
we call that carcinoma in situ.

175
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:42,160
Okay, key important question.

176
00:07:42,160 --> 00:07:44,560
What's the distinction between dysplasia and carcinoma?

177
00:07:44,560 --> 00:07:46,000
Why do we make that distinction?

178
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,640
Can you remember from the neoplasia lectures,

179
00:07:48,640 --> 00:07:52,480
what's the key feature that distinguishes dysplasia from carcinoma?

180
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:54,640
The answer is reversibility.

181
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:57,280
CIN 1 has the potential to reverse.

182
00:07:57,280 --> 00:07:59,920
CIN 2 has the potential to reverse.

183
00:07:59,920 --> 00:08:03,680
And CIN 3 very, very rarely could potentially reverse.

184
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:06,800
But once you have carcinoma in situ, there is no reversibility.

185
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:09,600
And carcinoma in situ, the next step will be invasion,

186
00:08:09,600 --> 00:08:13,600
producing a squamous cell carcinoma, an invasive squamous cell carcinoma.

187
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:18,800
Now remember that CIN 1, it reverses about 66% of the time.

188
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:22,640
And CIN 2 reverses about 33% of the time.

189
00:08:22,640 --> 00:08:25,280
But CIN 3 is very unlikely to reverse.

190
00:08:25,280 --> 00:08:31,680
So the more you progress in CIN, the less likely you are to reverse back to something

191
00:08:31,680 --> 00:08:34,080
less than the level at which you're at.

192
00:08:34,080 --> 00:08:38,720
So that's an important understanding that should be in our minds concerning CIN.

193
00:08:38,720 --> 00:08:43,920
And that is that we can divide, that is that we can subclassify the extent of dysplasia.

194
00:08:43,920 --> 00:08:47,040
Now here's a classic picture of CIN.

195
00:08:47,040 --> 00:08:50,960
And let me take you back a couple pictures and just show you the normal for a minute.

196
00:08:50,960 --> 00:08:54,960
So here was the normal, and I'm going to erase that to just show you that this is what the normal

197
00:08:54,960 --> 00:08:56,720
squamous epithelium looks like.

198
00:08:56,720 --> 00:08:59,920
Notice that the cells become pink as you go towards the top.

199
00:08:59,920 --> 00:09:02,960
They become smaller, and their nucleus becomes smaller.

200
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:07,520
Look at how tiny that nucleus is, for example, compared to the nucleus there.

201
00:09:08,240 --> 00:09:12,000
Now if we go back and look at the picture that I just showed you, look at these cells.

202
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:13,040
They're not becoming pink.

203
00:09:13,040 --> 00:09:14,080
They're really dark blue.

204
00:09:14,080 --> 00:09:16,880
Whatever's at the bottom looks exactly like what's at the top.

205
00:09:16,880 --> 00:09:20,160
So from top to bottom here, we've got dysplastic cells.

206
00:09:20,160 --> 00:09:24,320
Now basically, if I were to look here, I would say that this is carcinoma in situ,

207
00:09:24,320 --> 00:09:25,520
at this point here.

208
00:09:25,520 --> 00:09:29,120
Maybe if I were to look here, and I see a little bit of pinking of the cells,

209
00:09:29,120 --> 00:09:35,520
and a little bit of maturation, I might call that area CIN 3, just to highlight this notion.

210
00:09:35,520 --> 00:09:40,480
But the point is, when you look at an area like this, you've got these large blue cells

211
00:09:40,480 --> 00:09:41,840
from top to bottom.

212
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:44,800
You're really going to call this carcinoma in situ.

213
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:48,480
So that's what I want to highlight from this image present in your text.

214
00:09:48,480 --> 00:09:52,640
Now again, you know that CIN progresses stepwise, so that you go from 1 to 2,

215
00:09:52,640 --> 00:09:56,800
then 2 to 3, 3 to CIS, and eventually to invasive carcinoma.

216
00:09:56,800 --> 00:09:58,000
But it's not inevitable.

217
00:09:58,000 --> 00:09:59,760
As I told you, it may regress.

218
00:09:59,760 --> 00:10:03,520
In particular, CIN 1 regresses 66% of the time.

219
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:09,440
CIN 2 regresses about 33% of the time, and CIN 3 is very unlikely to regress,

220
00:10:09,440 --> 00:10:13,360
but is very likely to progress to carcinoma in situ.

221
00:10:13,360 --> 00:10:18,640
Now of course, once patients get carcinoma in situ, they'll eventually develop invasive carcinoma,

222
00:10:18,640 --> 00:10:21,360
and then we'd call that cervical carcinoma.

223
00:10:21,360 --> 00:10:25,200
Now the invasive carcinoma, the key feature is that it goes through the basement membrane.

224
00:10:25,200 --> 00:10:29,360
That's what distinguishes carcinoma in situ from invasive carcinoma.

225
00:10:29,360 --> 00:10:32,800
The invasive carcinoma usually arises in these patients

226
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:34,080
in a middle-aged woman.

227
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:38,720
And the idea here is that the HPV infection will occur early in the woman's life,

228
00:10:38,720 --> 00:10:40,480
like the age of 20 or 25.

229
00:10:41,280 --> 00:10:47,280
And it takes about 25 to 30 years before the patient actually develops a full-blown carcinoma.

230
00:10:47,280 --> 00:10:51,920
And that's because it takes time to go through this dysplasia carcinoma sequence.

231
00:10:51,920 --> 00:10:54,720
The classic presentation would be vaginal bleeding.

232
00:10:54,720 --> 00:10:59,440
The woman would present with either bleeding from the vaginal canal or post-coital bleeding,

233
00:10:59,440 --> 00:11:00,640
bleeding after intercourse.

234
00:11:00,640 --> 00:11:03,760
Those are two classic presentations for cervical carcinoma.

235
00:11:04,240 --> 00:11:06,480
What's the key risk factor for cervical carcinoma?

236
00:11:06,480 --> 00:11:07,680
We've already discussed it.

237
00:11:07,680 --> 00:11:10,000
It's high-risk HPV infection.

238
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:11,120
Now everybody knows that.

239
00:11:11,120 --> 00:11:14,640
You need to know again that the high-risk viruses are 16 and 18,

240
00:11:14,640 --> 00:11:17,280
and then 31 and 33 are also common.

241
00:11:17,280 --> 00:11:19,040
There are other ones.

242
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:20,480
They are a little less common.

243
00:11:20,480 --> 00:11:24,080
You need to know that the high-risk HPV produces E6 and E7.

244
00:11:24,080 --> 00:11:25,840
E6 knocks out P53.

245
00:11:25,840 --> 00:11:27,840
E7 knocks out retinoblastoma.

246
00:11:28,240 --> 00:11:30,480
So that's what I would say concerning the key risk factors.

247
00:11:30,480 --> 00:11:34,000
Now there are some secondary risk factors that will also increase the risk.

248
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,560
And there's two that you really should be aware of.

249
00:11:36,560 --> 00:11:37,760
The first is smoking.

250
00:11:37,760 --> 00:11:41,280
Now it's important to remember smoking here.

251
00:11:41,280 --> 00:11:45,520
Generally speaking, when I think about smoking, I think about the things that make sense.

252
00:11:45,520 --> 00:11:49,680
For example, obviously smoking is going to increase the risk of oropharyngeal carcinoma

253
00:11:49,680 --> 00:11:51,760
because the smoke hits the oropharynx.

254
00:11:51,760 --> 00:11:55,200
Obviously smoking would increase the risk of esophageal squamous cell carcinoma

255
00:11:55,200 --> 00:11:57,120
because the smoke hits the esophagus.

256
00:11:57,120 --> 00:12:00,320
Some of it goes down the throat when the patient's breathing in, for example.

257
00:12:00,960 --> 00:12:03,440
Obviously smoking would increase the risk of lung cancer

258
00:12:03,440 --> 00:12:04,400
because it goes to the lung.

259
00:12:05,280 --> 00:12:05,920
It makes sense.

260
00:12:05,920 --> 00:12:10,800
The smoking would increase the risk of kidney cancer and urothelial carcinoma

261
00:12:10,800 --> 00:12:14,240
because the kidney takes all of those carcinogens that go into the blood

262
00:12:14,240 --> 00:12:15,840
and it concentrates it in the urine.

263
00:12:15,840 --> 00:12:17,760
And so anything that the urine is going to touch

264
00:12:17,760 --> 00:12:21,200
is going to have an increased risk of cancer when a patient smokes.

265
00:12:21,200 --> 00:12:22,080
So those all make sense.

266
00:12:22,080 --> 00:12:26,400
But there's two that you kind of have to remember beyond those that make sense.

267
00:12:26,400 --> 00:12:28,960
One is cervix and the other is pancreas.

268
00:12:28,960 --> 00:12:32,720
So an important risk factor for cervical carcinoma is smoking.

269
00:12:32,720 --> 00:12:36,080
An important risk factor for pancreatic carcinoma is smoking.

270
00:12:36,080 --> 00:12:38,160
So that's what I would like to say about smoking.

271
00:12:38,160 --> 00:12:40,640
Now immunodeficiency is very high yield.

272
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:41,600
Why?

273
00:12:41,600 --> 00:12:46,240
Because remember that it's the vast majority of the time we actually use our immune system

274
00:12:46,240 --> 00:12:47,440
to destroy the virus.

275
00:12:47,440 --> 00:12:53,520
And so the immune system is essential in this disease to remove the essential cause,

276
00:12:53,520 --> 00:12:55,920
the initiator of the disease to begin with.

277
00:12:55,920 --> 00:13:01,360
And so patients who are immune deficient, they have an increased risk of developing cervical carcinoma.

278
00:13:01,360 --> 00:13:05,280
And interestingly, if you were to go and take a list of the AIDS-defining illnesses,

279
00:13:05,280 --> 00:13:10,640
remember that there are a series of illnesses that you can use to call a patient who has HIV as

280
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:11,760
now having AIDS.

281
00:13:11,760 --> 00:13:14,720
And those are called the AIDS-defining illnesses.

282
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:19,360
One of them on that list is cervical carcinoma.

283
00:13:19,360 --> 00:13:23,120
So that if a patient actually develops a cervical carcinoma with HIV,

284
00:13:23,120 --> 00:13:25,280
that's a potential AIDS-defining illness.

285
00:13:25,600 --> 00:13:28,960
And this is a very high yield point to be aware of.

286
00:13:29,920 --> 00:13:34,080
Okay, now remember that when patients get cervical carcinoma, they can actually get two types.

287
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:36,800
So we've got the exocervix and the endocervix.

288
00:13:36,800 --> 00:13:39,200
And you've got squamous cells in the exocervix.

289
00:13:39,200 --> 00:13:41,520
And then you've got columnar cells in the endocervix.

290
00:13:41,520 --> 00:13:44,400
So the patients can develop squamous cell carcinoma.

291
00:13:44,400 --> 00:13:47,360
And they can also develop an adenocarcinoma.

292
00:13:47,360 --> 00:13:48,880
Squamous is much more common.

293
00:13:49,520 --> 00:13:51,360
Adeno is a little bit less rare.

294
00:13:51,360 --> 00:13:52,800
It's a little bit less common.

295
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:57,840
But what I really want you to remember is that both are associated with HPV.

296
00:13:58,400 --> 00:14:01,760
Patients who get squamous cell carcinoma, it is driven by HPV.

297
00:14:01,760 --> 00:14:06,960
But patients who get adenocarcinoma, it is also driven by HPV.

298
00:14:06,960 --> 00:14:08,320
And examiners like to ask you,

299
00:14:08,320 --> 00:14:10,160
which of the following is due to HPV?

300
00:14:10,160 --> 00:14:13,680
Squamous cell carcinoma, squamous cell and adenocarcinoma, etc.

301
00:14:13,680 --> 00:14:18,000
And so you need to know that adenocarcinoma of the cervix is also driven by HPV.

302
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:24,080
Another very important principle concerning cervical cancer is that the tumor tends to grow locally

303
00:14:24,080 --> 00:14:27,040
and doesn't metastasize until very, very late.

304
00:14:27,040 --> 00:14:34,320
And so the classic findings are when you have an advanced tumor, one of the classic findings is

305
00:14:34,320 --> 00:14:35,440
hydronephrosis.

306
00:14:35,440 --> 00:14:40,880
Now, the idea here is that the tumor sort of bores through the anterior uterine wall into the bladder.

307
00:14:40,880 --> 00:14:43,920
And when it gets into the bladder, it blocks the ureters.

308
00:14:43,920 --> 00:14:49,920
And it's the blocking of the ureters that results then in hydronephrosis with eventual post renal failure.

309
00:14:49,920 --> 00:14:55,120
And the most common cause, or I should say one of the most common causes of death in patients with cervical cancer,

310
00:14:55,120 --> 00:14:58,720
is post renal failure with hydronephrosis.

311
00:14:58,720 --> 00:15:05,360
So we should be well aware of the fact that the cancers of the cervix, one of the important characteristics

312
00:15:05,360 --> 00:15:12,400
of these cancers is that they tend to locally create symptoms rather than metastasizing to a distant site.

313
00:15:12,400 --> 00:15:19,600
And that's the mechanism by which patients can expire with this cancer, very high yield to be aware of.

314
00:15:19,600 --> 00:15:25,280
Remember that one of the ways by which we can avoid the whole issue of cervical cancer is screening.

315
00:15:25,280 --> 00:15:32,720
And screening is essential, and the goal of screening in the context of cervix is to catch that dysplasia before it becomes cancer.

316
00:15:32,720 --> 00:15:38,080
Remember that I told you that patients normally get infected at about the age of 25 or, you know, 20,

317
00:15:38,080 --> 00:15:44,720
and it takes about 10 to 20 years, in most cases 20 years, before you actually develop that carcinoma.

318
00:15:44,720 --> 00:15:49,200
And so we've got this 20-year window where we can actually catch the patients with dysplasia

319
00:15:49,200 --> 00:15:51,680
and remove that dysplasia before it becomes cancer.

320
00:15:51,680 --> 00:15:56,240
And the way by which we find that dysplasia is called the pap smear.

321
00:15:56,240 --> 00:16:04,240
So the pap smear is the gold standard for screening, and it is the most successful cancer screening test developed to date.

322
00:16:04,240 --> 00:16:09,280
Now the idea here is that you take a brush, you go to the transformation zone,

323
00:16:09,280 --> 00:16:13,760
and you scrape away the cells using that brush, and then you put them on a slide,

324
00:16:13,760 --> 00:16:16,320
and you look at those cells under the microscope.

325
00:16:16,320 --> 00:16:22,800
Under the microscope, we look for dysplasia, and when we see dysplasia, we see characteristic features of malignant cells.

326
00:16:22,800 --> 00:16:32,800
For example, we see cells with dark nuclei and high NC ratios, and this is one of the key screening tests for cervical cancer.

327
00:16:32,800 --> 00:16:37,360
All right, so here's a picture of cytology, and this is a picture of a pap smear.

328
00:16:37,360 --> 00:16:43,840
Basically, this is a normal squamous cell, and these are the malignant cells, and you can clearly see that,

329
00:16:43,840 --> 00:16:47,840
compare the nuclear size, this nucleus is tiny, this nucleus is huge.

330
00:16:47,840 --> 00:16:53,840
This cell has a very low NC ratio with a lot of cytoplasm and a little bit of nucleus.

331
00:16:53,840 --> 00:16:59,040
This cell has a very high NC ratio, a lot of nucleus, a very little bit of cytoplasm.

332
00:16:59,040 --> 00:17:05,520
The nucleus looks dark and hyperchromatic, and so, again, these are all the features that we would expect to see in dysplasia,

333
00:17:05,520 --> 00:17:08,640
and that's important to bear in mind.

334
00:17:08,640 --> 00:17:13,520
Now, when a patient has an abnormal pap smear, remember that the pap smear is a screening test,

335
00:17:13,520 --> 00:17:19,520
and every screening test in medicine must be confirmed, and so the confirmatory test is called colposcopy,

336
00:17:19,520 --> 00:17:28,960
where we basically put in a magnifying glass, introduce a magnifying glass into the vaginal canal and view the cervix,

337
00:17:28,960 --> 00:17:34,960
and when we view the cervix, we can apply acid to the cervix, and we can then detect areas of abnormality and biopsy those,

338
00:17:34,960 --> 00:17:38,720
and so when a patient has an abnormal pap smear, the next step will be biopsy,

339
00:17:38,720 --> 00:17:46,480
and biopsy will then be sent to the pathologist, and the pathologist will then determine the extent of dysplastic cells.

340
00:17:46,480 --> 00:17:51,600
There are some limitations to the pap smear, and examiners like to go after those limitations.

341
00:17:51,600 --> 00:17:56,160
First of all, if a pap smear is performed, but it's not done at the transformation zone,

342
00:17:56,160 --> 00:18:01,200
then the screening would be inadequate, you would not have actually tested the area that you wanted to test,

343
00:18:01,200 --> 00:18:06,560
and so that would result in a false negative test, so that's one limitation of the pap smear.

344
00:18:06,560 --> 00:18:11,920
Another limitation of the pap smear is that it does not detect adenocarcinoma very well.

345
00:18:11,920 --> 00:18:18,480
Now remember that we have a dysplastic sequence where we had CIN1, CIN2, CIN3 carcinoma in situ,

346
00:18:18,480 --> 00:18:23,200
and then invasive squamous cell carcinoma, but that applied to squamous lesions.

347
00:18:23,200 --> 00:18:30,640
And adenocarcinoma, it's much more difficult to detect, and it doesn't go through that same sequence, obviously.

348
00:18:30,640 --> 00:18:34,560
And so there is a limited efficacy in screening for adenocarcinoma.

349
00:18:34,560 --> 00:18:39,760
Now what's interesting is that despite the pap smear, all the great things that we have to say about the pap smear,

350
00:18:39,760 --> 00:18:42,160
that all applies to squamous cell carcinoma.

351
00:18:42,160 --> 00:18:47,120
And despite the pap smear, the incidence of adenocarcinoma has not decreased significantly.

352
00:18:47,120 --> 00:18:52,320
We're highlighting, again, the general principle that the pap smear is good for squamous cell carcinoma

353
00:18:52,320 --> 00:18:59,200
and its precursor lesions, but is not good for adenocarcinoma or its precursor lesions.

354
00:18:59,840 --> 00:19:02,320
The last point that I want to make concerns immunizations.

355
00:19:02,320 --> 00:19:06,320
Remember that, number one, immunization is effective in preventing HPV infection.

356
00:19:06,320 --> 00:19:08,160
That's the current understanding.

357
00:19:08,160 --> 00:19:12,160
The current immunization that we use, or the vaccine, is a quadrivalent vaccine.

358
00:19:12,160 --> 00:19:13,680
It covers four subtypes.

359
00:19:13,680 --> 00:19:14,320
Important to know.

360
00:19:14,320 --> 00:19:16,160
You've got to know these four subtypes.

361
00:19:16,160 --> 00:19:19,280
Six and eleven, they protect against the formation of condyloma.

362
00:19:19,280 --> 00:19:27,200
And sixteen and eighteen protect against the formation of CIN or VAIN or VIN, right?

363
00:19:27,200 --> 00:19:30,880
Dysplasia of the lower genital tract, especially CIN.

364
00:19:30,880 --> 00:19:34,640
Now sixteen and eighteen are high-risk subtypes, and they are the most common high-risk

365
00:19:34,640 --> 00:19:38,720
subtypes involved in that dysplastic carcinoma sequence.

366
00:19:38,720 --> 00:19:43,200
Protection lasts for about five years, so you have to re-immunize every five years.

367
00:19:43,200 --> 00:19:45,680
And remember that the patient still must get pap smears.

368
00:19:45,680 --> 00:19:46,080
Why?

369
00:19:46,080 --> 00:19:47,760
Why do they still need to get pap smears?

370
00:19:47,760 --> 00:19:50,880
The answer is because you're only protecting against sixteen and eighteen.

371
00:19:50,880 --> 00:19:55,440
What about thirty-one, thirty-three, and the other less common subtypes that could also

372
00:19:55,440 --> 00:19:56,880
result in dysplasia?

373
00:19:56,880 --> 00:20:02,400
So we have not eliminated by a vaccine the possibility of dysplasia.

374
00:20:02,400 --> 00:20:06,960
Therefore, patients still must undergo pap smear, and that's the high-yield principle

375
00:20:07,840 --> 00:20:08,880
to be aware of.

376
00:20:08,880 --> 00:20:12,080
So that closes off our section, and we're going to move up the tract next and go to

377
00:20:12,080 --> 00:20:22,080
endometrium and myometrium.

